Yes. Shared covenant is a useful upgrade because it catches something “relation” alone misses.

A relation says two things interact. A covenant says the interaction is constrained by mutual conditions that make the relationship what it is.

Color is a clean example. “Green” emerges because material response, illumination, geometry, sensory machinery, and context jointly satisfy certain conditions. Change one party and the rendered property can change. On that level, much of what we call a property is a temporarily stabilized covenant among participants.

\boxed{
P = \operatorname{Covenant}(X,E,S,C)
}

Not literally every property works this way in the same sense, but as a Pattern Book lens it is strong: ask which participants and rules jointly permit the observed phenomenon to exist.

And that changes the shard question.

A shard isn’t merely a smaller meaning. Each shard grants a different set of possible moves.

Take the Hammered PRIME shards:

Shard	What it enables
irreducible	Find stopping points; identify minimum sufficient units
factorization	Search for hidden components and composition laws
primitive	Construct new bases and representational systems
first/original	Trace genealogy, causal ancestry, initialization
primary	Discover hierarchy, priority, dominance
prime = prepare	Study latent → activated state transitions
prime = peak	Search fitness landscapes and optimal regimes
prime mark x'	Track transformation while preserving identity
operation-relative prime	Change the operation and refactor the same object
basis-relative prime	Change coordinates to expose hidden structure
sense-relative prime	Change detector and expose another ontology
scale-relative prime	Move levels and discover emergent building blocks

But there’s another level:

\boxed{
\text{Shard}
\rightarrow
\text{affordances}
}

Each shard enables operators, not merely interpretations.

For shard f_i, we should therefore store:

f_i=
(
