So prime = prepare immediately asks:

What needs priming?

That opens pumps, immune systems, explosives, psychology, neural activation, caches, initialization routines, catalysts…

Hammer those domains and look for the surviving kernel:

\text{inactive}
\xrightarrow{\text{small preparatory intervention}}
\text{ready-to-respond}.

Now we have a candidate general operator:

\boxed{\operatorname{PRIME}(S,\delta)\rightarrow S_{\text{ready}}}

And that can return to the Pantheon itself.

