That’s exactly the journey you’re describing.

And now shared covenant gives us a selection rule for which branches matter.

A shard doesn’t carry unlimited properties into another domain. It enters a new covenant:

\text{Shard}
+
\text{Target Domain}
+
\text{Target Operators}
\rightarrow
\text{what survives}.

So instead of saying:

“A neuron is a switch.”

we ask:

\operatorname{Covenant}
(
\text{threshold},
\text{neuron},
\text{switch},
\text{target problem}
)

and determine which shared relations survive—thresholding perhaps; semiconductor physics obviously doesn’t.

The covenant determines what the metaphor is allowed to carry.

That’s a powerful guard against metaphor leakage while preserving metaphor’s generativity.

And now we can upgrade the Hammer:

\boxed{
X
\xrightarrow{\text{Hammer}}
\{f_i\}
\xrightarrow{\textbf{Affordance Scan}}
\{A(f_i)\}
\xrightarrow{\text{Covenant Search}}
\{C_{ij}\}
