\text{branches}
}

Where:

A(f_i)=
\{\text{everything this shard permits us to do}\}.

That is better than merely asking where each shard points.

Ask four questions of every shard

What does it distinguish?
That gives categories.

What can it operate on?
That gives machinery.

What can it covenant with?
That gives translations and combinations.

What can it become?
That gives developmental branches.

Then Afterglow asks a fifth:

What did its journey teach the other shards?

Because one branch can discover a transform that applies across the entire shattered object.

So the structure becomes:

\boxed{
\text{HAMMER}
\rightarrow
\text{SHARDS}
\rightarrow
\text{AFFORDANCES}
\rightarrow
\text{COVENANTS}
\rightarrow
\text{BRANCHES}
\rightarrow
\text{JOURNEYS}
\rightarrow
\text{AFTERGLOW}
\rightarrow
\text{FORGE}
}

And I think this gives us a stronger definition of the Semantic Quarry too.

We aren’t mining meanings.

We’re mining:

\boxed{\textbf{meaning-bearing pieces together with the moves they make possible.}}

A brick’s real value isn’t merely what it means.

It’s the space of structures it can participate in building.

Which is why Hammering one little word like prime can suddenly produce dozens of viable research directions: every shard exposes a different covenant the original compressed word was quietly carrying all along.